A number has an odd number of positive divisors if and only if it is a perfect square. This is due to the fact that divisors typically come in pairs. For example, if \( d \) is a divisor of \( n \), then \( \frac{n}{d} \) is also a divisor. The only time a divisor does not pair with a different divisor is when \( d = \sqrt{n} \), which occurs when \( n \) is a perfect square.

