### Mathematical Concepts
The exponent of a prime \( p \) in \( n! \) can be calculated using the formula:

\[
\text{Exponent of } p \text{ in } n! = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^k} \right\rfloor
\]

This formula counts how many multiples of \( p \), \( p^2 \), \( p^3 \), etc., are present in the numbers from \( 1 \) to \( n \).

### Step-by-Step Solution
1. **Identify \( n \) and \( p \)**: Here, \( n = 109 \) and \( p = 3 \).
